    "engine": "jupyter",
    "markdown": "---\ngd-head-tags:\n  - >-\n    `<script type=\"application/ld+json\">[{\"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n    \"@type\": \"SoftwareSourceCode\", \"name\": \"EdgeCase\", \"description\":\n    \"High-performance graph analysis library\", \"codeRepository\":\n    \"https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase\", \"license\":\n    \"https://opensource.org/licenses/MIT\", \"url\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase\", \"documentation\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase\", \"programmingLanguage\": {\"@type\":\n    \"ComputerLanguage\", \"name\": \"Python\"}}, {\"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n    \"@type\": \"WebPage\", \"name\": \"Graph.is_k_regular - EdgeCase\", \"isPartOf\":\n    {\"@type\": \"WebSite\", \"name\": \"EdgeCase\"}, \"url\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase/reference/Graph.is_k_regular.html\"}]</script>`{=html}\nopen-graph:\n  title: >-\n    [Graph.is_k_regular()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n    .doc-label-method} | EdgeCase\n  description: Check if the graph is k-regular (all nodes have degree k).\ntwitter-card:\n  title: >-\n    [Graph.is_k_regular()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n    .doc-label-method} | EdgeCase\n  description: Check if the graph is k-regular (all nodes have degree k).\ntitle: >-\n  [Graph.is_k_regular()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n  .doc-label-method}\nbody-classes: doc-api-page doc-py-api-page\nshift-heading-level-by: 0\nbread-crumbs: false\ntoc-depth: 3\ndescription-meta: Check if the graph is k-regular (all nodes have degree k).\npage-navigation: false\nhtml-table-processing: none\n---\n\n::: {.doc-subject}\n\nCheck if the graph is k-regular (all nodes have degree `k`).\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-usage-source}\nUsage\n\n[Source](https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase/blob/main/python/edgecase/graph.py#L28462-L28587){target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"}\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-signature .doc-function}\n```pythonsignature\nGraph.is_k_regular(k)\n```\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-text}\n\n![Is k-regular](../assets/graphs/is-k-regular.light.svg){.lightbox fig-alt=\"The same six-node cycle on both sides, with badges showing every node has degree 2. Left: asking for k=2 matches every node, so is_k_regular(k=2) returns True. Right: asking for k=3 matches none of them (badges in the accent color), so is_k_regular(k=3) returns False even though the graph is regular.\" width=\"100%\"}\n\nA graph is k-regular if every node has degree exactly `k`. For directed graphs, this means\nall nodes have in-degree `k` and out-degree `k`.\n\nThis is `is_regular()` with the shared degree pinned to a specific value, which is useful\nwhen a particular structure is expected, for example:\n\n- checking that a ring is `2`-regular or a cubic (`3`-regular) design came out as intended\n- verifying the output of a random-regular-graph generator\n- confirming that every node has exactly `k` partners in a matching-style design (e.g.\n  every participant reviews and is reviewed by `k` others)\n\nDegrees are computed from all edges: parallel edges each count, and in an undirected graph\na self-loop adds `2` to its node's degree (in a directed graph it adds `1` to both the\nin-degree and out-degree). The whole graph is checked and the current selection is\nignored.\n:::\n\n## Parameters {.doc-parameters}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code>[k]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [int]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   The required degree, a non-negative integer. In an undirected graph every node must\n    have exactly `k` incident edge ends; in a directed graph every node must have both\n    in-degree and out-degree equal to `k`. An empty graph is `k`-regular only for `k=0`.\n:::\n\n## Returns {.doc-returns}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code> [bool]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   `True` if the graph is k-regular, `False` otherwise.\n:::\n\n## Technical Notes {.doc-technical-notes}\n\nThis is the stricter cousin of `is_regular()`: instead of asking \"is every degree\nequal?\", it pins down the exact value $k$. Empty graphs vacuously satisfy $k = 0$;\ncycles are $2$-regular; the complete graph $K_n$ is $(n - 1)$-regular; and paths are\nnever regular because their endpoints have degree $1$ while interior nodes have degree\n$2$. On directed graphs the check tightens to both $\\deg^-(v) = k$ and $\\deg^+(v) = k$\nfor every $v$.\n\n## See Also {.doc-see-also}\n\n[](`~is_regular`) \n\n:   Whether every node has the same degree.\n\n[](`~add_random_regular_graph`) \n\n:   Add a random graph in which every node has degree d.\n\n[](`~degrees`) \n\n:   Get the degree of every node.\n\n## Examples {.doc-examples}\n\nA cycle's nodes each have degree `2`, so it satisfies `k=2` but no other value of `k`:\n\n::: {#9bd070f2 .cell execution_count=1}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nimport edgecase as ec\n\ngraph = ec.Graph().add_cycle_graph(6)\n\ngraph.is_k_regular(2), graph.is_k_regular(3)\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=1}\n```\n(True, False)\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\nNodes with no edges at all have degree `0`, so an all-isolated graph is 0-regular but\nnothing else:\n\n::: {#c902a7d2 .cell execution_count=2}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\ngraph = ec.Graph().add_nodes([\"A\", \"B\", \"C\"])\n\ngraph.is_k_regular(0), graph.is_k_regular(1)\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=2}\n```\n(True, False)\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\nIn a directed graph both directions must match `k`. A directed cycle gives each node one\nincoming and one outgoing edge, so it is `1`-regular, not `2`-regular, even though each node\ntouches two edges:\n\n::: {#197d3002 .cell execution_count=3}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\ndirected_cycle = ec.Graph(directed=True).add_cycle_graph(n=4)\n\ndirected_cycle.is_k_regular(1), directed_cycle.is_k_regular(2)\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=3}\n```\n(True, False)\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\n### Same size, different spread\n\nA random 3-regular graph on 12 nodes has 18 edges. A random graph with the same 12 nodes\nand 18 edges has the same average degree, 3, but nothing holds each node to it:\n\n::: {#8674bce0 .cell execution_count=4}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nregular = ec.Graph().add_random_regular_graph(n=12, d=3, seed=1)\nchance = ec.Graph().add_gnm_graph(n=12, m=18, seed=2)\n\n\ndef degrees(graph):\n    return sorted(graph.degrees(return_dict=True).values())\n\n\n(\n    (regular.is_k_regular(3), degrees(regular)),\n    (chance.is_k_regular(3), degrees(chance)),\n)\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=4}\n```\n((True, [3, 3, 3, 3, 3, 3, 3, 3, 3, 3, 3, 3]),\n (False, [1, 1, 1, 2, 2, 2, 3, 3, 4, 5, 5, 7]))\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\nThe regular graph on the left and the random graph on the right, each node labeled by its\ndegree:\n\n::: {layout-ncol=\"2\"}\n\n::: {#81b985a3 .cell execution_count=5}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nregular.plot(\n    label_by=\"degree\", seed=1, width=320, height=320, legend=False\n)\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=5}\n```{=html}\n<div class=\"ec-theme\" style=\"display:contents\"><style class=\"ec-theme-vars\">:where(.ec-theme){--ect-3239c383:#fafafa;--ect-e5dfd507:#94a3b8;--ect-8a2de1d4:#374151;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(15,23,42,0.88);--ect-21ade003:#f1f5f9;--ect-61169ea5:#c2410c;--ect-f59ba8df:#a1a1aa}@media (prefers-color-scheme: dark){.ec-theme{--ect-3239c383:#0d1117;--ect-e5dfd507:#4b5563;--ect-8a2de1d4:#d1d5db;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(241,245,249,0.92);--ect-21ade003:#0f172a;--ect-61169ea5:#f97316;--ect-f59ba8df:#71717a}}:is(html.quarto-light, body.quarto-light, html[data-bs-theme=\"light\"], [data-jp-theme-light=\"true\"], body.vscode-light, body.vscode-high-contrast-light, html[theme=\"light\"]) .ec-theme{--ect-3239c383:#fafafa;--ect-e5dfd507:#94a3b8;--ect-8a2de1d4:#374151;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(15,23,42,0.88);--ect-21ade003:#f1f5f9;--ect-61169ea5:#c2410c;--ect-f59ba8df:#a1a1aa}:is(html.quarto-dark, body.quarto-